From: deltav7 fork
Subject: [PATCH] tui: stop @opentui/core eating ctrl+a inside dialog filter inputs

Fixes: ctrl+a in the model-selection dialog moves the cursor (visible as the accent
       coloured cursor cell jumping to column 0) instead of opening the provider list.

Upstream base: anomalyco/opencode @ f96e6aa6ed3f0a9c027da4eb7c4fba248426152b
Verified against: @opentui/core@0.4.5 (bun cache copy on this box)

ROOT CAUSE (all line numbers read from shipped code, not guessed)

  1. packages/tui/src/config/keybind.ts:116
         model_provider_list: keybind("ctrl+a", "Open provider list from model dialog")
     -> command "model.dialog.provider" (keybind.ts:370)

  2. packages/tui/src/ui/dialog-model.tsx registers that action; dialog-select.tsx:298
     hands its bindings to the keymap via useBindings().

  3. dialog-select.tsx:501 renders <input> = InputRenderable and focuses it (ref, :512).

  4. packages/tui/src/keymap.tsx:175
         function hasManagedTextareaFocus(renderer) {
           const editor = renderer.currentFocusedEditor
           return editor instanceof TextareaRenderable && !(editor instanceof InputRenderable)
         }
     gates registerManagedTextareaLayer (keymap.tsx:229).  With an InputRenderable
     focused this is FALSE, so opencode's own "input.line.home" layer is NOT active and
     is NOT the culprit.  (The second ctrl+a default, input_line_home at keybind.ts:170,
     is a red herring.)

  5. The real consumer is @opentui/core itself.  InputRenderable does not override
     handleKeyPress, so it inherits TextareaRenderable's (index.bun.js:6528):

         handleKeyPress(key) {
           if (this.traits.suspend !== true) {
             const action = getKeyBindingAction(this._keyBindingsMap, key)
             if (action) {
               const handler = this._actionHandlers.get(action)
               if (handler) return handler()      // <-- consumed, returns true
             }
           }
           ...
           return false
         }

     and its table (defaultTextareaKeyBindings, index.bun.js:6370) contains

         { name: "a", ctrl: true, action: "line-home" }

     The editor runs before the keymap, consumes ctrl+a for line-home, and
     "model.dialog.provider" is never dispatched.

     So "the cause is upstream" was correct, but the collision is
     opencode-keymap vs @opentui/core's hard-coded editor table - NOT a collision
     between the two ctrl+a fields inside keybind.ts.

WHY THE FIX IS SHAPED THIS WAY

  Passing a filtered copy of the table does nothing: both the constructor
  (index.bun.js:6461) and the setter (:6654) call
      mergeKeyBindings(defaultTextareaKeyBindings, this._keyBindings)
  so the defaults are always the base layer and an entry can never be deleted.
  mergeKeyBindings (chunk-bun-tkm837n2.js:4332) keys on
      `${name}:${ctrl}:${shift}:${meta}:${super}`
  and lets the custom entry REPLACE the default one.  Replacing ctrl+a with an action
  core has no handler for makes handleKeyPress fall through to `return false`, which is
  exactly "unhandled" - the key then travels on to the keymap and the dialog action runs.

  Scope: only DialogSelect's filter input.  The main prompt textarea is untouched, so
  ctrl+a there still does input.line.home.  ctrl+shift+a, alt+a and cmd+a (select-all)
  keep core's defaults - verified by running the predicate against the real table.
  Inside the filter box, Home still reaches buffer-home (index.bun.js:6366).

diff --git a/packages/tui/src/ui/dialog-select.tsx b/packages/tui/src/ui/dialog-select.tsx
--- a/packages/tui/src/ui/dialog-select.tsx
+++ b/packages/tui/src/ui/dialog-select.tsx
@@ -1,9 +1,10 @@
 import {
   InputRenderable,
   RGBA,
   ScrollBoxRenderable,
   TextAttributes,
+  type KeyBinding,
   type KeyEvent,
   type Renderable,
 } from "@opentui/core"
 import type { Binding } from "@opentui/keymap"
@@ -19,5 +20,21 @@
 import { getScrollAcceleration } from "../util/scroll"
 import { useTuiConfig } from "../config"
 import { formatKeyBindings, useBindings, useKeymapSelector } from "../keymap"

+// @opentui/core's editors carry their own hard-coded key table
+// (defaultTextareaKeyBindings) and TextareaRenderable.handleKeyPress() consumes a match
+// before the keymap is ever consulted. ctrl+a is "line-home" in that table, so while
+// this filter input holds focus it swallowed any dialog action bound to ctrl+a - which
+// is what keybind.ts ships as `model_provider_list`, "Open provider list from model
+// dialog". Symptom: the cursor jumps to column 0 in the accent `cursorColor` and the
+// provider list never opens.
+//
+// mergeKeyBindings() keys entries on `name:ctrl:shift:meta:super` and lets a custom
+// entry replace the default, but never delete it - so we replace ctrl+a with an action
+// core has no handler for. handleKeyPress() then falls through and returns false, the
+// event continues to the keymap, and the dialog's own ctrl+a action runs. Other a-chords
+// (ctrl+shift+a, alt+a, cmd+a) keep core's defaults, and Home still does buffer-home.
+const FILTER_KEY_BINDINGS = [
+  { name: "a", ctrl: true, action: "opencode:pass-through" },
+] as unknown as KeyBinding[]
+
 export interface DialogSelectProps<T> {
@@ -506,6 +523,7 @@
                   props.onFilter?.(e)
                 })
               }}
+              keyBindings={FILTER_KEY_BINDINGS}
               focusedBackgroundColor={theme.backgroundPanel}
               cursorColor={theme.primary}
               focusedTextColor={theme.textMuted}
